   153	### Update behaviour (replay)
   154	
   155	`hivemind.py replay --cutoff 2026-06-30` builds research from sources known by
   156	the cutoff, then feeds later sources in publication order through `update`
   157	(extract → route to premises → isolated edge judgments → monitors → thesis
   158	refresh for new mechanisms). With the final configuration (`evals/bench-v1/results/replay2-report.json`): 33 theses at the cutoff; the
   159	16 later sources were routed into 488 new evidence edges (257 supports, 37
   160	contradicts, 163 contextual, 31 irrelevant), 43 thesis revisions, 1 retirement and 7
   161	new theses for mechanisms the cutoff research did not cover, all with change records
   162	for the timeline. Example: the TechCrunch "robotaxis are moving in reverse" article
   163	(2026-07-23) produced 21 claims, 20 routed links and 7 contradicting edges against
   164	the robotaxi thesis. Monitors could not resolve
   165	organically in the replay because the corpus jumps from FY2025 10-Ks to Q2-2026
   166	reports (no Q1-2026 reports); the matcher correctly refused to use Q2 figures for
   167	Q1 monitors. Resolution is therefore measured as a component benchmark on real claims:
   168	`evals/bench-v1/run_monitors.py` retargets each of the final DB's 79 quarterly
   169	monitors to the company's latest reported quarter, runs the production matcher,
   170	and three blind judges checked every answer against the full candidate list.
   171	Result: 65 matches, **0 wrong** (every value checked against the source's own
   172	columns), 3 misses (bounds such as "less than 1%" that the matcher declined to
   173	read as numbers), 11 correct "no match" answers. The threshold comparison and
   174	kill-rule evaluation after a match are deterministic Python.
