   195	### 2.2 How links are weighted, and which links carry information
   196	
   197	- **Direct customer–supplier links with a known share carry information.**
   198	  - [Cohen & Frazzini (2008)](https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/~afrazzin/pdf/Economic%20Links%20and%20Predictable%20Returns%20-%20Cohen%20and%20Frazzini.pdf)
   199	    measure link strength as the share of the supplier's sales that goes to the
   200	    customer (mean 19.8%). News about customers predicts supplier returns
   201	    (1.55% per month long–short), and prices take up to six months to absorb it.
   202	  - The effect grows with the share and with how specific the input is. Disaster
   203	    shocks to suppliers of specific inputs hurt customers, while shocks to
   204	    suppliers of generic inputs don't
   205	    ([Barrot & Sauvagnat](https://academic.oup.com/qje/article-abstract/131/3/1543/2461213)).
   206	- **Direction of spread.** Demand shocks travel upstream, to suppliers; supply
   207	  shocks travel downstream, to customers
   208	  ([Acemoglu, Akcigit & Kerr](https://cepr.org/voxeu/columns/networks-and-macroeconomic-shocks)).
   209	- **Two-step effects are real but smaller.** After the 2011 Japan earthquake,
   210	  firms two steps away lost about 72–75% as much sales growth as direct
   211	  partners
   212	  ([Carvalho et al. 2021](https://vasco-m-carvalho.github.io/pdfs/JapanEQ.pdf)).
   213	  A path's strength is therefore better computed as the product of the shares
   214	  along it than with a fixed per-step discount.
   215	- **The sign of competitor links varies.** A bankruptcy usually hurts rivals
   216	  (contagion) but can help them in concentrated industries
   217	  ([Lang & Stulz 1992](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/0304405X9290024R)).
   218	- **Co-movement is not an economic link.** Index membership, shared fund
   219	  ownership and ETF arbitrage all create co-movement that later reverses
   220	  ([Barberis et al.](https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/~jwurgler/papers/wurgler_barberis_shleifer.pdf),
   221	  [Antón & Polk](https://personal.lse.ac.uk/polk/research/ConnectedStocks.pdf)).
   222	  These signals are diagnostics, never evidence for a premise.
   223	- **Text-based peers beat fixed industry codes.**
   224	  - [Hoberg–Phillips TNIC](https://hobergphillips.tuck.dartmouth.edu/idata/Readme_tnic3.txt)
   225	    compares the product-description text of 10-Ks.
   226	  - The vertical version
   227	    ([Frésard, Hoberg & Phillips 2020](https://bfi.uchicago.edu/wp-content/uploads/vertical_integration_fresard_hoberg_phillips.pdf))
   228	    gives each pair of companies an upstream score and a downstream score.
   229	  - Peers generated with GPT predict correlated fundamentals better than
   230	    10-K-similarity or SIC peers
   231	    ([Cao et al. 2025](https://ideas.repec.org/a/spr/reaccs/v30y2025i4d10.1007_s11142-025-09892-6.html)).
   232	  - Being close in text is not the same as being economically connected.
   233	    *Preprint:* when an LLM filtered candidate links found by embedding
   234	    similarity, the Sharpe ratio rose from 0.742 to 0.820
   235	    ([Huang et al. 2026](https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.19476)).
   236	- **Commercial alpha from customer momentum has decayed since publication.**
   237	  FactSet reports a Sharpe ratio of 0.38 for 2006–2025 (vendor research). We use
   238	  links to route evidence to theses, not as a trading signal, so this decay
   239	  matters less for us.
   240	
   241	### 2.3 Sector and theme layer
   242	
   243	- **Sector (one label per company):**
   244	  - [GICS](https://www.spglobal.com/spdji/en/documents/methodologies/methodology-gics.pdf)
   245	    assigns the sub-industry that earns more than 60% of revenue, with a fallback
   246	    when no activity reaches that.
   247	  - [FactSet RBICS](https://assets.ctfassets.net/lmz2w5z92b9u/67nHF3Io7Zg8Ka1eQSqWsi/73277f7a9bc6250c727c8625bdc55164/factset_rbics_methodology_guide.pdf)
   248	    maps each reported segment to one of 1,603 product-level sub-industries,
   249	    with that segment's revenue share (Apple: iPhone 62.8%, Services 14.0%, and
   250	    so on).
   251	- **Theme exposure is a share of revenue, and a model classifies but doesn't
   252	  score.**
   253	  - [MSCI Relevance Score](https://www.msci.com/eqb/methodology/meth_docs/MSCI_Relevance_Score_Calculation_Methodology.pdf)
   254	    = (revenue of theme-matched segments + d × revenue of related segments) ÷
   255	    total revenue. Index inclusion requires a score of at least 25%.
   256	  - MSCI's LLM version
   257	    ([blog](https://www.msci.com/research-and-insights/blog-post/leveraging-language-models-to-capture-investment-strategies))
   258	    requires a "Positive" agent and a "Skeptic" agent to both accept a business
   259	    line. Code then sums that line's revenue share.
   260	  - [Bloomberg's thematic protocol](https://assets.bbhub.io/professional/sites/10/Bloomberg-Thematic-Protocol.pdf)
   261	    tiers revenue exposure as more than 50%, 20–50%, or less than 20%.
   262	
   263	### 2.4 How LLM ingestion pipelines are built today
   264	
   265	Most current systems use the same four steps:
   266	
   267	1. An LLM with a schema extracts candidate entities and links from each chunk.
   268	2. Code checks each link against a closed list of types and the source → target
   269	   pairings each type allows.
   270	3. Entity resolution finds a short candidate list (embeddings plus BM25), and a
   271	   model decides among those candidates.
   272	4. Duplicates are merged, and links a newer source contradicts are marked
   273	   invalid.
   274	
   275	The systems differ mainly in how they handle time and strength.
   276	
   277	- **Microsoft GraphRAG**
   278	  ([prompt](https://github.com/microsoft/graphrag/blob/main/packages/graphrag/graphrag/prompts/index/extract_graph.py),
   279	  [walkthrough](https://tech.bertelsmann.com/en/blog/articles/how-microsoft-graphrag-works-step-by-step-part-12))
   280	  asks the model for a 1–10 relationship strength and sums it across chunks.
   281	  LightRAG adds 1 for every chunk that mentions a link
   282	  ([code](https://github.com/HKUDS/LightRAG/blob/main/lightrag/operate.py)).
   283	  In both, a fact that is simply repeated grows stronger. Model-stated
   284	  confidence scores are poorly calibrated
   285	  ([2412.14737](https://arxiv.org/html/2412.14737v2)).
   920	  1. Belief core.
   921	  2. Crash-safe loop.
   922	  3. Live Q3 run.
   923	  4. Belief timeline view.
   924	  5. Narratives, as beliefs about theme entities.
   925	  6. Follow, then M8.
