evals/bench-v1/blind/theses/judgment-meta.json:39:    "A": "Both theses cover one nascent option (enterprise compute and agents) that currently has no disclosed revenue, so relevance to the stock is low. There are no metrics, thresholds or dated horizons. Invalidation is near-tautological ('a source correction showing Meta did not state this'). The same Zuckerberg 'foolish to sell all compute' quote is reused as evidence for unrelated premises, including the net-economics one. Premise 2 calls the agent offering a plan but ignores that Meta actually launched a business AI agent in June 2026, and the attached quote does not show outcome-based pricing. A has no bear case and never mentions ads, capex/FCF, legal, Reality Labs or tax. Its checks rely on disclosures Meta may never break out.",
evals/bench-v1/blind/theses/judgment-meta.json:40:    "B": "Several falsifiers are poorly calibrated. The FCF invalidation (FY2026 FCF >= $43.6B) is nearly unreachable given the $130-145B capex guide, so the thesis is almost unfalsifiable. The ad-revenue invalidation (<12%) sits far below the growth implied by Q3 guidance ($61-64B), and its label wrongly uses the impression-growth rate as the threshold for revenue growth. The Reality Labs falsifier is knife-edge: a loss slightly below $19.193B would 'invalidate' a thesis that the segment remains a substantial drag. The tax invalidation (>= 29.6%) is very loose and mixes remaining-quarter guidance with a full-year metric. The legal monitor compares a full FY2027 figure with a single-quarter charge and cannot be checked next quarter. The FTC antitrust judgment and the separate FTC privacy-order matter are grouped without distinction. B omits the enterprise AI and compute upside, $131B of contractual commitments and the $45.95B data-center JV exposure, the pending DMA assessment of the less-personalised-ads option and its EU revenue risk, and the headcount reduction and $165-169B expense guide as an earnings driver."
