evals/bench-v1/blind/theses/judgment-nvidia.json:23:  "rationale": "B covers the things that actually move NVIDIA's earnings: supply-constrained ~70% growth, gross-margin trajectory, balance-sheet-supported AI-lab demand and customer concentration, inventory and purchase-obligation write-down risk, China foreclosure, and platform or networking attach. Each thesis has dated numeric monitors, such as the Q3 FY27 revenue floor of $105.84B (108 minus 2%), DSO of 60 days, $4B of inventory provisions and the 71-72% gross-margin trough, that can be checked against the next 10-Q. A is accurate and epistemically careful, but its invalidation criteria mostly test whether a statement was made rather than whether the investment view is right. It has no thresholds or dates, gives a whole thesis to a small CPU adjacency, and leaves out headline growth, margins, concentration and capital allocation. B has real errors that need fixing: it assigns the 70% guide to the wrong year, misreads a supply statement, relies on stale China evidence and misattributes the margin driver. Even so, a PM could use it and could not use A.",
evals/bench-v1/blind/theses/judgment-nvidia.json:28:      "Thesis 1 FY2028 monitor: the 65% threshold is labelled 'the prior fiscal-year growth rate', but 65% is FY2026 growth, not FY2027. It is also below the ~70% FY2028 guide, so it does not test the guided outcome.",
evals/bench-v1/blind/theses/judgment-nvidia.json:30:      "Thesis 2: treats the FY26 10-K statement 'no H200 revenue to date' as current and sets 'China DC compute revenue > 0 in FY2027' as the invalidation trigger. The reference says H200 shipments to China under US licenses were already <1% of Q2 FY27 data-center revenue, so the thesis's own monitor may already be triggered. It also has no materiality threshold and omits the BIS case-by-case licensing change.",
evals/bench-v1/blind/theses/judgment-nvidia.json:39:    "A": "Invalidation criteria are mostly 'a source correction showing NVIDIA did not make this statement', which tests attribution rather than the thesis. There are no numeric thresholds, and horizons read 'date unknown' even though the Q3 guide and print timing are known. The CPU thesis covers a business of about $5B trailing revenue against a run-rate near $400B, and its $20B demand figure and FY28 'more than double' premise have no attached support. The China thesis blames US license terms when the binding constraints are Chinese import policy and an outlook that already assumes zero China DC revenue, so it has little incremental earnings effect. It does not cover core DC growth or the 70% FY28 guide, gross margin or memory costs, the 22%/14% customer concentration, the AI-lab demand backed by NVIDIA's balance sheet, custom-silicon competition, or the $40.4B of buybacks.",
evals/bench-v1/results/relations-codex-r1.json:457:    "reason": "Amazon's release reports that its chips business exceeded the $25 billion annual revenue run rate, but the supplied text does not specify when that threshold was reached. The July 30 publication date does not establish that it had been surpassed by mid-2026."
