evals/bench-v1/blind/theses/tesla.md:84:### Thesis 2: Cybercab-specific data and confidence gates could constrain paid Robotaxi scaling  [bear]
evals/bench-v1/blind/theses/tesla.md:85:- Statement: If the stated Cybercab-specific data and confidence gates remain binding and materially delay paid deployment, without equivalent capacity from other vehicles, Tesla’s paid autonomous-service scale and revenue opportunity could be constrained. The supplied reports do not establish a binding delay, its duration, its cause, or a revenue impact.
evals/bench-v1/results/qa-v4-full-test.json:1940:     "quote": "NVIDIA provides a take-or-pay commitment on a \nportion of the facility's capacity, a minimum revenue guarantee that gives lenders the confidence to underwrite the \nproject, and in exchange, we share in a portion of the neoclouds revenue earned above that floor.",
evals/bench-v1/results/qa-v4-full-test.json:2557:   "text": "NHTSA questioned whether Recall 23V838 effectively addressed the underlying defect: preliminary analysis found 20 crashes in vehicles with the remedy installed, spanning all 3 crash types identified in its investigation. Those crashes comprised 9 frontal-plane strikes involving a vehicle, object, or person in the travel path, 6 yaw, spin, or understeer crashes in low-traction environments, and 5 inadvertent-steering-override crashes. In preliminary testing with the camera obscured, NHTSA’s Vehicle Research and Test Center could not identify a difference in the initiation of the driver-warning cascade before versus after the remedy, so ODI said it would evaluate whether the remedy resolved the defect and whether its warnings were adequate. NHTSA also flagged remedy permanence: Tesla changed the sensitivity and sample window for increased attentiveness requirements, while single-pull Autopilot activation was not the default for vehicles receiving the field remedy, had to be enabled through a menu, could readily be enabled or disabled, and could be changed while driving. ODI planned to evaluate the reasons and effects of changes to the remedy, and to assess the timing, drivers, field-performance impacts, and exclusion rationale for Tesla’s related non-remedy updates.",
evals/bench-v1/results/qa-v4-full-test.json:2581:     "text": "In preliminary testing with the camera obscured, NHTSA’s Vehicle Research and Test Center could not identify a difference in the initiation of the driver-warning cascade before versus after the remedy, so ODI said it would evaluate whether the remedy resolved the defect and whether its warnings were adequate.",
evals/bench-v1/results/qa-v4-full-test.json:2645:     "text": "NHTSA’s Vehicle Research and Test Center was unable to identify a difference in the initiation of the driver-warning cascade between pre-remedy and post-remedy conditions with the camera obscured.",
evals/bench-v1/blind/theses/judgment-alphabet.json:23:  "rationale": "B covers the variables that move GOOGL: AI answers eroding Search click and ad economics, the capex-to-FCF squeeze (Q2 FCF -$5.9B, capex doubled to $44.9B), DOJ search and ad tech remedies, Cloud's AI-driven growth and margin, and how durable Services is. It gives numeric monitors that map to real 10-Q disclosures (paid clicks, TTM FCF/OCF, segment margins). A has only two heavily hedged theses. One is a generic Cloud demand-and-capacity statement with no thresholds; the other is an accounting-comparability point about the Q2 equity gain that any PM already adjusts for. A's 'restatement' invalidations and 'date unknown' horizons make it close to unfalsifiable. B has real flaws: invalidation thresholds are too loose (0% growth for an 82% grower), the antitrust monitor is an off-target proxy, and it misses the equity raise and dilution. Even so, B is clearly the more usable research.",
evals/bench-v1/blind/theses/judgment-alphabet.json:32:      "Thesis 4: the confirm monitor says 'above the FY2025 growth rate' but uses a 35% threshold. The reference gives FY2025 Cloud growth as 36%."
evals/bench-v1/blind/theses/judgment-alphabet.json:40:    "A": "Only two theses. It ignores Search and ads (>70% of revenue), the risk that AI disrupts click economics, DOJ search and ad tech remedies, capex and FCF pressure ($91.4B FY2025, capex doubling in Q2 2026, negative Q2 FCF), and the $242.8B backlog. It sets no thresholds and gives no dated horizon. Invalidation for historical premises is 'source restatement', which is not falsification. Thesis 2 is close to tautological: it never quantifies that the gain is ~$6.26 of $9.11 EPS, so its 'operating offset insufficient' premise is obviously true and uninformative. The same single quote is reused across premises, with no opposing evidence.",
evals/bench-v1/blind/theses/judgment-alphabet.json:41:    "B": "Invalidation thresholds are too loose and contradict the theses' own statements: Cloud is 'invalidated' only at <=0% growth although the thesis claims above-FY2025 (~36%) growth, and Services only at <=0% although it claims double-digit growth. The Cloud margin floor of 25% is far below the current ~36%. The capex monitor (FY2026 > $91.4B) is almost certain to trigger and does not test the harm. The antitrust monitor (accrued legal balance vs $15.6B) is a poor proxy for how remedies affect distribution and data economics. The antitrust thesis omits the pending DOJ ad tech structural remedies, the one-year limit on revenue-share terms and the extension of remedies to Gemini/GenAI. Neither the Services thesis nor the AI-search bear thesis cites Search & other +17% in Q2 2026 (key disconfirming or supporting data). It misses the $49.6B equity raise, the $40B ATM and debt issuance (dilution and a shift in capital return), the EPS distortion from the equity gain, the Wiz/Intersect deals and Other Bets losses."
