evals/bench-v1/blind/theses/nvidia.md:120:- Mechanism: NVIDIA describes a capacity take-or-pay commitment, a minimum revenue guarantee and participation above a revenue floor, while stating that independent capital underwrites deals and that NVIDIA is not making loans. System inference: this can support financing and hardware demand but may transfer some utilization or revenue risk to NVIDIA. The contractual triggers, purchases received in return, committed amounts and realized returns are not supplied; take-or-pay obligations need not arise only below a rental-revenue threshold.
evals/bench-v1/blind/theses/nvidia.md:125:  - Evidence (supports): NVIDIA describes a revenue-sharing structure with neoclouds in which it provides a take-or-pay commitment on a portion of the facility's capacity, a minimum revenue guarantee that gives lenders confidence to underwrite the project, and in exchange shares in a portion of the neoclouds revenue earned above that floor; NVIDIA says it is not making loans and that independent capital still underwrites every deal on its own merits.
evals/bench-v1/blind/theses/nvidia.md:133:  - Evidence (insufficient): NVIDIA describes a revenue-sharing structure with neoclouds in which it provides a take-or-pay commitment on a portion of the facility's capacity, a minimum revenue guarantee that gives lenders confidence to underwrite the project, and in exchange shares in a portion of the neoclouds revenue earned above that floor; NVIDIA says it is not making loans and that independent capital still underwrites every deal on its own merits.
evals/bench-v1/blind/theses/nvidia.md:150:  - Evidence (supports): NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said the supply commitments and capacity NVIDIA had aligned gave the company confidence in its growth and revenue.
evals/bench-v1/blind/theses/microsoft.md:100:- Monitor (confirms): Growth above this threshold would indicate that Azure demand is continuing to support the broader cloud franchise. — Microsoft Cloud revenue growth gt 20 percent (yoy_growth), FY2027 Q1 (quarter ending Sep 2026)
evals/bench-v1/blind/theses/microsoft.md:101:- What would prove it wrong: Azure growth falls below the invalidation threshold while Microsoft Cloud growth also materially slows, showing that current demand and backlog are not converting into sustained growth.
evals/bench-v1/blind/theses/microsoft.md:165:- Monitor (confirms): Growth above this threshold would support continued premium monetization of the installed commercial base. — Microsoft 365 Commercial cloud revenue growth gt 15 percent (yoy_growth), FY2027 Q1 (quarter ending Sep 2026)
evals/bench-v1/blind/theses/microsoft.md:168:- What would prove it wrong: Microsoft 365 Commercial cloud growth falls below the stated invalidation threshold and paid Copilot seats fail to exceed the reported FY2026 level, indicating that AI upsell is not offsetting modest seat growth.
evals/bench-v1/results/qa-legacy-dev.json:959:     "text": "If consumer-accepted payments do not reach the required threshold by February 2027, Amazon will provide additional automatic payments to consumers who previously received refunds under the settlement; consumers who have accepted payments will be eligible to receive an additional $149, for a total of",
evals/bench-v1/results/qa-legacy-dev.json:960:     "quote": "If consumer-accepted payments do not reach the required threshold by February 2027, Amazon will provide additional automatic payments to consumers who previously received refunds under the settlement. As part of the revised order, consumers who have accepted payments will be eligible to receive an a",
evals/bench-v1/blind/theses/judgment-microsoft.json:23:  "rationale": "Set A covers the real debate on the stock: Azure and the backlog ($678B RPO, Azure growth of 41% for the year and 43% in Q4) against the AI capex burden (Cloud gross margin down to 66%, $115.9B of capex, $329.1B of leases not yet started), plus M365/Copilot upsell and the CMA, IRS and More Personal Computing risks. Its monitors have numeric thresholds and dates (e.g. Azure above 35% confirms and below 30% invalidates for FY27 Q1), and its numbers match the reference. Set B has three bull theses and no bear case, even though the capex, margin, IRS, CMA-SMS and partner-concentration evidence calls for one. Two of its three theses rest on UK-only CMA findings, read as bullish, that matter little to the stock. Its invalidation criteria are mostly 'a source correction' with no metrics and dates marked 'unknown'. Both sets miss the OpenAI exposure ($24.1B of revenue, $6.0B of receivables, about a 25% stake, and a $5.0B net gain that lifted EPS), but only Set A would give a PM something to check next quarter.",
evals/bench-v1/blind/theses/judgment-microsoft.json:26:      "Thesis 5 monitor: 'Microsoft 365 Copilot paid seats gt 30 count' has the wrong unit. The reference figure is over 30 million paid seats, so as written the threshold is 30 seats and would trigger trivially.",
evals/bench-v1/blind/theses/judgment-microsoft.json:41:    "A": "Leaves out the OpenAI exposure entirely ($24.1B of commercial revenue, $6.0B of receivables, an equity-method stake of about 25%, a $5.0B net-income gain) and the risk that partner consumption, which the 10-K flags, does not materialize. Also skips earnings quality (the $3.2B Anthropic gain in Q4) and the power/grid constraint. Some monitors are weak proxies: gross unrecognized tax benefits against the $25.8B level barely indicates IRS outcome risk and tends to grow on its own; a 'binding remedy count' ignores size; 66% is a rounded knife-edge threshold for Cloud GM. The CMA thesis applies UK-only findings to Azure broadly without estimating how much revenue is exposed. The MPC bear case is low-stakes given the segment's rising operating income.",
evals/bench-v1/blind/theses/judgment-microsoft.json:42:    "B": "No bear thesis despite strong evidence for one (capex up $51.4B, Cloud GM down to 66%, $329.1B of leases, $28.9B IRS NOPAs, partner-concentration and power risks). Two of three theses are UK-market CMA findings read as bullish. They leave out the CMA's recommendation to prioritise SMS investigations (importance 3 in the reference), which is the predicate for remedies. That makes the framing one-sided, and the UK market is too small to move the stock. Thesis 2 says 'retain customers despite limited competitive pressure', which inverts its own logic. Horizons are 'date unknown', there are no numeric thresholds, and invalidation for the historical premises is a 'source correction', which is not falsifiable in any investment sense. Thesis 1 never links cloud revenue to margins or earnings. The above-cost-returns premise is duplicated across Theses 2 and 3."

