evals/bench-v1/results/qa-v4-final-test.json:2608:   "text": "NHTSA questioned whether Recall 23V838 effectively addressed the underlying defect: preliminary analysis found 20 crashes in vehicles with the remedy installed, spanning all 3 crash types identified in its investigation. Those crashes comprised 9 frontal-plane strikes involving a vehicle, object, or person in the travel path, 6 yaw, spin, or understeer crashes in low-traction environments, and 5 inadvertent-steering-override crashes. In preliminary testing with the camera obscured, NHTSA’s Vehicle Research and Test Center could not identify a difference in the initiation of the driver-warning cascade before versus after the remedy, so ODI said it would evaluate whether the remedy resolved the defect and whether its warnings were adequate. NHTSA also flagged remedy permanence: Tesla changed the sensitivity and sample window for increased attentiveness requirements, while single-pull Autopilot activation was not the default for vehicles receiving the field remedy, had to be enabled through a menu, could readily be enabled or disabled, and could be changed while driving. ODI planned to evaluate the reasons and effects of changes to the remedy, and to assess the timing, drivers, field-performance impacts, and exclusion rationale for Tesla’s related non-remedy updates.",
