evals/bench-v1/blind/theses-final/judgment-meta.json:23:  "rationale": "B addresses what actually drives Meta's stock: capex-driven FCF collapsing to $784M, ad momentum (+14% impressions, +12% price per ad), youth and EU legal exposure, and Reality Labs losses. Its numbers are mostly accurate, carry the right periods and come with dated Q3 2026 monitors. However, its lead bear thesis blames the Q2 2026 operating-income decline on infrastructure while ignoring $3.58B of legal and severance charges, and several of its invalidation thresholds sit far outside guided or base levels, so they can almost never trigger. A is carefully hedged and makes few factual errors, but it only covers optional upside from enterprise compute and agents. It treats the June 2026 agent launch as merely a plan and has no quantitative or dated monitors. It also says nothing about ads, capex/FCF, legal exposure or Reality Labs.",
evals/bench-v1/blind/theses-final/judgment-meta.json:111:        "detail": "The invalidation monitor 'ad revenue growth < 10% YoY in Q3 2026' is far below what the Q3 revenue guide of $61-64B implies. Against a prior-year Q3 base of about $51B (the derived Q2 2025 base is ~$47.5B), the guide implies roughly 19-25% growth, so the invalidator is practically unreachable without a large guidance miss. The confirm threshold of 20% or more sits inside the guided range and is largely pre-signalled."
evals/bench-v1/results/relations-v4-r1.json:425:    "reason": "The company release reports 26% year-over-year growth for Advertising in Q2 2026, above the premise's 20% threshold."
evals/bench-v1/blind/theses-dev-v2v3/judgment-meta.json:23:  "rationale": "Both sets quote the reported numbers accurately and consistently. Examples: FoA revenue = ads 59,363 + other 1,007; Q2 operating income of 18,775 vs 20,441 = -8%; the RL margin of (870)% = 19,193 / 2,207. B has broader coverage, including tax normalization, the $131B of commitments, price per ad and the Q3 guide. However, B's monitor design fails as a PM decision tool. Several B theses are confirmed by construction or cannot be falsified: capex >= the guided floor, other revenue > its FY2025 level, ETR <= 17% and an unreachable FCF >= FY2025 invalidator. B also uses one threshold to both confirm and invalidate three times and mislabels the Reality Labs monitor. In addition, B attributes the Q2 operating-income decline to AI investment, although $3.58B of legal and severance charges explain it. B also presents lapping a one-time tax charge as an earnings tailwind. A has its own weak spots: the FCF confirmation is near-certain, the RL break-even invalidator is unreachable and the legal thesis has no invalidator. Even so, A's core tests are real uncertainties: capex above the $137.5B midpoint, Q3 revenue against the $62.5B midpoint and other-revenue growth against 50%.",
evals/bench-v1/blind/theses-dev-v2v3/judgment-meta.json:76:        "detail": "The 17% threshold comes from the 15-17% outlook for the REMAINING QUARTERS of 2026, but it is applied to the FULL-YEAR FY2026 ETR. The full-year guide is 13-16%, so its ceiling is 16%. A 16.5% full-year rate would breach full-year guidance yet 'confirm' the thesis."
evals/bench-v1/blind/theses-dev-v2v3/judgment-meta.json:80:        "type": "monitor_same_threshold",
evals/bench-v1/blind/theses-dev-v2v3/judgment-meta.json:81:        "detail": "The thesis confirms at FY2026 ETR <= 17% and invalidates at ETR > 17%, so one threshold does both jobs."
evals/bench-v1/blind/theses-dev-v2v3/amazon.md:53:- Mechanism: AI-led infrastructure and fulfillment capital expenditure → Higher infrastructure and fulfillment outlays increase capital intensity and depreciation before new capacity is fully monetized; that delays free-cash-flow recovery and raises the return threshold for Amazon's investment program. → AI and other capacity investment continues to consume cash faster than Amazon's operating cash-flow growth can replenish it, keeping free cash flow weak through the investment cycle.
